  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Friday)\nIssued at 1133 AM MDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nWestern CONUS upper ridge will shift east into the Northern \nPlains and support hot and dry weather as a stronger upper trough \nmoves across the northern CONUS. This upper trough will support a \nstrong cold front into the region Monday, bringing a brief mid \nweek cool down before the heat returns. Upper level ridge will \ncontinue to tilt into the region today ahead of a strong southern \nCanadian upper trough. This will will support WAA as the ll \nthermal ridge pushes east. Haze from modified wildfire smoke will \ncontinue to impact the area, supporting elevated pollution levels.\nSfc trough will advect SE and settle into the region Sunday. \nBreezy southerly winds can be expected ahead the trough with winds\nshifting NW behind the trough. CAA will be delayed until early \nMonday morning as the main cold front will push into the FA. \nHence, hot temps still expected Sunday even behind the trough. \nExpect the hottest temps in southwest through central SD where \n100-110 will be expected for highs. Heat adv area still looks good\nas dry air will limit the highest heat index readings to this \narea. Cold front will move through early Monday with winds gusting\nover 40 mph through much of Monday morning on the SD Plains. \nWinds will decrease through afternoon as the main pressure rise \nbubble exits the FA. A batch of thick low level smoke is expected \nto push through the region behind the front Sunday night into \nMonday per HRRR/RAP forecasts. This may impact visibility esp \nMonday morning. Conds should improve through Monday as the ll \ntrajectories indicate a more northerly airmass origin. Still could\nsee a few showers and TS across mainly scentral SD late Monday \nnight as the jet entrance region will overlap with a weak impulse \npassing to the south of the FA, supporting potential elevated \nconvection. This has been trending more south in recent model \nruns. Temps will fall into the 40s and 50s Monday night with a few\n30s expected in portions of the higher Black Hills. Highs Tues \nwill be 10 to 15 degrees below seasonal norms mainly in the 70s, \nwith 60s in the Black Hills. Western ridge will rebuild behind the\ndeep upper low slowly pushing east across interior Canada. This \nwill support a warm up as SE LL return flow ensues. Expect highs \nto be back in the 90s by Thur. Increasing LL moisture will support\nisolated TS chances by Wed as weak impulses will advect through \nthe region in NW flow.",
